* fix(compression): wait out the deterministic fallback instead of the LLM idle window [daveey]

Problem: test_over_window_request_commits_the_deterministic_fallback_on_the_first_stall
failed its first CI attempt (job 123475790090) with out is live. Logs: deterministic
fallback "produced no compression" after "no progress for 6.4s, ceiling 2.0s". Retry passed.

Why 1: the pinned deterministic retry was aborted and handed back the caller's list
(conversation_compression.py "produced no compression" when result_msgs is messages).
Why 2: that retry inherited the over-window collapsed ceiling, which the stall test
sets to the 2s LLM idle window. The no-LLM commit does not touch_progress, so one
silent window aborts it. Wall clock was 6.4s against a 2.0s ceiling.
Why 3: the idle window is the hung-socket detector. The deterministic rung is local
work (prompt rebuild, SQLite, malloc_trim) with no socket and no heartbeats.
Why 4: commit 401122f7f3c widened the shared budget 0.4s to 2.0s instead of splitting
the two waits, so the next throttled runner missed it again.

Root (happened): one wall-clock budget serves hung-socket detection and local commit
completion. Root (not caught): the test that asserts the commit uses a budget the
commit can miss, and CI retry greened the job.

The deterministic pinned retry now waits for the worker. 120s is only a hang
backstop; the wait returns as soon as the commit lands. A SIGSTOP/SIGCONT duty
cycle (15ms run / 400ms stop) reproduces the CI assertion on the old code and
passes on this one. The stall-interrupted comment was shortened so the file stays
at its main line cap.

